 If your storage provider is on a different site than your Fizzy instance and doesn't return CORS headers on presigned URL responses, inline images may fail to load.
 In that case, set `SERVICE_WORKER_CORS_ENABLED=false` so the service worker fetches uploaded files without CORS mode.
 
+#### Push notifications (optional)
+
+Fizzy can push whatever lands in someone's notifications tray to their phone and desktop through their web browser.
+People turn this on for each device from their Notification Settings.
+
+Push notifications need a VAPID key pair, which identifies your instance to the browsers' push services.
+Generate one from a Fizzy checkout with:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails runner 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}", "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"'
+```
+
+Or use any other VAPID key generator, such as `npx web-push generate-vapid-keys`.
+Then set both keys:
+
+```sh
+docker run --env VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=BNc... --env VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=Y2f... ...
+```
+
+Push notifications stay off until both are set.
+Keep using the same pair once people have turned push on: browsers tie each subscription to the key it was created with, so after a change, each device picks push up again only once its owner revisits their Notification Settings.
+
+Fizzy only delivers to the push services of Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge, so it needs outbound HTTPS access to them.
+Like webhooks, it looks their addresses up through public DNS resolvers (1.1.1.1 and 8.8.8.8).
+
 #### Multi-tenant mode
 
 By default, when you run the Fizzy Docker image you'll be limited to creating a single account (although that account can have as many users as you like).
